-- title:
--   The cone of a configuration at a point is open
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be a real inner product space. A *configuration* $\Gamma$ on $E$ assigns to each point $x \in E$ a double cone
--   $$\Gamma(x) \;=\; V(v,\theta) \;=\; \{h \in E : h \neq 0,\ \angle(v,h) < \theta \text{ or } \angle(v,h) > \pi - \theta\}$$
--   with unit axis $v$ and apex angle $\theta \in (0,\pi/2]$, and one writes
--   $$V^\Gamma[x] \;=\; x + \Gamma(x) \;=\; \{y \in E : y - x \in \Gamma(x)\}$$
--   for that cone translated so that its tip sits at $x$. Then for every configuration $\Gamma$ and every $x \in E$, the set $V^\Gamma[x]$ is open in $E$.
--
--   Openness of the shifted cones is the topological input behind the arguments that select an intermediate point inside two cones and then keep a whole neighbourhood of it: an open set has positive measure around each of its points, which is exactly what averaging arguments need. Note that $x \notin V^\Gamma[x]$, since $0$ never belongs to a double cone.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The statement is proved for an arbitrary real inner product space $E$, not only for $\mathbb{R}^d$, and requires no hypothesis on $\Gamma$ beyond its being a configuration — the apex bounds are already built into the type of double cones.
